  Hamiltonian-path count=5

[6] Working hypotheses produced by this scout
  H1. Even perfect numbers are the n=2 unit-excess product chain a/(2a-1) with a power of two and 2a-1 prime.
  H2. LRC14's a/(14a-1) chain is the deficient prime-q shadow of that chain, with defect 12/(14a-1).
  H3. F3/F4 is the first useful seam: 2/3 gives product 6 and K_{2,3}; 3/4 gives the first reduced K33 wall.
  H4. The graph-product ledger is valid only with minor labels attached; edge counts and mediants are too lossy.
  H5. Tournament vertices should be proof obligations/carrier roles here, not runners, because the question is which quotient preserves the obstruction predicate.
